+### 5.3 `--channel paper`: Thesis Nonreciprocal Formula Channel
 
-For ordinary thesis experiments, primarily use `obs` and `action`. The code also retains two advanced ablation channels:
+```powershell
+--channel paper
+```
+
+The total torque follows the thesis equation:
+
+```text
+tau_i = -kappa * delta_theta_i
+        + kappa_alpha_i * (delta_theta_(i+1) - delta_theta_(i-1))
+```
+
+The fixed passive term `-kappa*delta_theta_i` is implemented by the environment's torsional spring, and the policy network outputs only one learnable coefficient, `kappa_alpha_i`. Defaults:
+
+```text
+--passive-kappa 4.0
+--max-control-gain 9.0
+```
+
+In the environment, this corresponds to:
+
+```text
+observation_func = dth_tot
+control_mode     = nonreciprocity
+action dimension = 1
+```
+
+### 5.4 `--channel k2_positive`: Fix k1 and Constrain k2 to Remain Positive
+
+```powershell
+--channel k2_positive
+```
+
+The environment computes:
+
+```text
+tau_i = fixed_k1 * [theta(i+1)-theta(i-1)]
+        + k2_i * F * theta_dot_i
+k2_i > 0
+```
+
+The policy outputs only `k2_i`. The action-space lower bound defaults to `1e-3`, so this is not merely `k2 >= 0`; the sign remains strictly positive.
+
+### 5.5 `--channel k2_negative`: Fix k1 and Constrain k2 to Remain Negative
+
+```powershell
+--channel k2_negative
+```
+
+The environment computes:
+
+```text
+tau_i = fixed_k1 * [theta(i+1)-theta(i-1)]
+        + k2_i * F * theta_dot_i
+k2_i < 0
+```
+
+The default fixed arguments are:
+
+```text
+--fixed-k1 -5.0
+--min-k2-magnitude 1e-3
+--max-control-gain 9.0
+```
+
+`--fixed-k1 -5.0` is the default control value and can be changed on the command line for an ablation or grid search. Both signed-k2 channels have action dimension `1`.
