 ## 15. Implementation Description Suitable for the Thesis
 
-This project extends the original deformable-robot reinforcement-learning framework with a unified interface for switching robot morphology, terrain, control channel, and reinforcement-learning algorithm. Robot morphologies include the open-chain crawler and closed-loop ring; terrains include flat ground, stairs, and a mesh-based tunnel environment. The control channels include direct torque and formula action. The direct-torque channel observes the adjacent-angle difference `theta(i+1)-theta(i-1)` and has the policy network output joint torque directly. The formula-action channel uses the same angle difference, but the policy network outputs two control coefficients, `k1` and `k2`; during physical integration the environment computes the actual torque in real time as `tau = k1*(theta(i+1)-theta(i-1)) + k2*F*theta_dot`. The training script supports the continuous-control methods PPO and DDPG and uses the environment action spec to match the different action dimensions of direct-torque and formula-action modes automatically. All key configurations are saved in checkpoint metadata, allowing the demonstration script to restore the training robot, terrain, channel, control mode, and algorithm automatically and thereby keep training and simulation consistent.
+This project extends the original deformable-robot reinforcement-learning framework with a unified interface for switching robot morphology, terrain, control channel, and reinforcement-learning algorithm. Robot morphologies include the open-chain crawler and closed-loop ring; terrains include flat ground, stairs, and a mesh-based tunnel environment. Five main control channels are implemented: direct-torque `obs`; `action`, which learns `k1/k2` jointly; the thesis nonreciprocal formula `paper`; and `k2_positive` and `k2_negative`, which fix `k1` and constrain `k2>0` and `k2<0`, respectively. In the `paper` channel, the environment implements the fixed passive term `-kappa*delta_theta_i`, while the policy network outputs the active nonreciprocal coefficient `kappa_alpha_i`. Each signed-k2 channel learns one sign-constrained `k2_i`, with actual torque `tau = fixed_k1*(theta(i+1)-theta(i-1)) + k2_i*F*theta_dot_i`. The training script supports the continuous-control methods PPO and DDPG and uses the environment action spec to select one- or two-dimensional action outputs automatically. All key configurations, including `fixed_k1`, `min_k2_magnitude`, and `passive_kappa`, are saved in checkpoint metadata, allowing the demonstration and analysis scripts to restore the training configuration automatically.
 
